                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The **GEO Stack** is a five-layer framework for **Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)** that explains how content becomes visible, extractable, and citeable in AI-driven search systems.[1][2][4] In the version developed by **The GEO Lab / Artur Ferreira**, the layers are **Retrieval Probability, Extractability, Entity Reinforcement, Structural Authority, and System Memory**.[2][4][17]\n\nIn practical terms, the framework asks:\n- **Can AI find the content?** \u2192 *Retrieval Probability*.[4][10]\n- **Can AI parse and quote it cleanly?** \u2192 *Extractability*.[4][10]\n- **Does the system recognize the brand/entity consistently?** \u2192 *Entity Reinforcement*.[2][4]\n- **Does the page/site signal trust and authority?** \u2192 *Structural Authority*.[2][4]\n- **Does the content remain remembered and referenced over time?** \u2192 *System Memory*.[2][4]\n\nThe GEO Lab describes it as a **diagnostic and optimization model** for identifying where content fails in the AI retrieval pipeline and what to improve at each stage.[8][10] Some other writers use \u201cGEO stack\u201d to mean different, proprietary frameworks with other layer names, so the term is **not standardized** across the industry.[3][6][15]",
